<SubSection title="Convection" id="Convection">
<p>Convective heat transfer is the transport of thermal energy due to bulk motion of a fluid (liquid or gas) over a surface. </p>

<b><DisplayEquation equation="q_{1-2} = hA(T_1 - T_2)" title="Newton's Law of Cooling" background="True"/></b>

where 
<Itemize>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="q_{1-2}" /> = rate of heat flow from 1 (hotter) to 2 (colder) <InlineEquation equation="[W]" /></Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="A" /> = surface area normal to the direction of heat flow <InlineEquation equation="[m^2]" /></Item> 
<Item><InlineEquation equation="T_1" /> = temperature of 1 (e.g., system) <InlineEquation equation="[K]" /></Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="T_2" /> = temperature of 2 (e.g., surroundings) <InlineEquation equation="[K]" /></Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="h" /> = convective heat transfer coefficient (which always includes the effect of conduction in the fluid) <InlineEquation equation="[\\frac{W}{m^2 K}]" /></Item>
</Itemize>

<p> The convective heat transfer coefficient <InlineEquation equation="h"/> is a proportionality constant between the temperature difference and heat flux.
Note that it is not a material property, but depends on geometry, flow conditions, and fluid properties, which can all be measured experimentally. </p> 

<p> Convection can be <b>free</b> (driven by differences in density) or <b>forced</b> (driven by external forces such as pumps and fans). </p>


